  "narrative": "## Narrative\n\nThe RaMP enrichment analysis of the 11 differentially-abundant metabolites identifies two overwhelmingly dominant pathways, both carrying an FDR of 0.0 and extraordinary fold-enrichments above 950×: **Galactose Metabolism (KEGG map00052 / SMP00043)** and the disease correlate **Galactosemia (SMP00182)**. Six of the 11 metabolites — galactitol (C01697), myo-inositol (C00137), galactinol (C01235), D-glucose (C00221), sucrose (C00089), and glycerol (C00116) — fall within these related modules, making the galactose / polyol axis the central biological story.\n\n**Driver compounds.** Galactitol (the reduced product of galactose via aldose reductase) is a pathognomonic marker of galactose intolerance and classical galactosemia, and its elevation here is consistent with a block or stress in downstream galactose utilization. Galactinol (the galactosyl donor for raffinose-family oligosaccharide synthesis) and myo-inositol (a downstream product of galactose metabolism and a major osmolyte) are co-enriched, reinforcing a unified metabolic cluster. The presence of sucrose and glycerol alongside D-glucose indicates that sugar-homeostasis pathways are broadly perturbed, possibly reflecting hepatic or renal stress responses.\n\n**Secondary pathway.** The Glycerophospholipid biosynthetic pathway (WP2533, FDR 7.9 × 10⁻⁵) captures myo-inositol, ethanolamine (C00189), and glycerol (C00116), consistent with inositol's role as a precursor for phosphatidylinositol synthesis. A separate Reactome module associates dopamine (C03758) with neuronal synaptic pathways, suggesting that neurotransmitter dynamics may be secondarily affected — though this does not reach genome-wide significance relative to the primary carbohydrate signal.\n\n**Biological significance.** The convergence on galactose metabolism implies that the treatment group experiences either impaired galactose catabolism or a compensatory accumulation of polyol intermediates, a pattern reported in metabolic liver disease, diabetic nephropathy, and infantile galactosemia. The glycerol and inositol co-enrichment may reflect systemic osmotic adjustment in response to elevated sugar alcohols. Dopamine dysregulation, if confirmed in follow-up studies, could indicate neuroendocrine consequences of the underlying metabolic stress.\n\n**Upstream/downstream context.** Within the galactose cluster, D-glucose (C00221) is upstream of galactose metabolism via the Leloir pathway; galactitol (C01697) is downstream of galactose reduction and upstream of downstream sugar alcohol clearance routes. The myo-inositol (C00137) membership spans both the galactose and glycerophospholipid modules, placing it at a branch point between carbohydrate and membrane-lipid metabolism. These relationships are grounded in confirmed pathway annotation and co-occurrence plausibility scores of 1.0 across all six galactose-cluster metabolites.",
